   "text": "Submitted successfully. Summary of the search:\n\n**Process:**\n1. **Declared triggers**: stop if best Sharpe > 1.2, stop if \u22652 failures, stop if last gain \u2264 0.02\n2. **Prior pick**: nominal anchor on feature 1 (no genuine domain prior available for anonymized features)\n3. **Content moves** (3 of 3 used):\n   - `init` \u2192 feature 7 (long), Sharpe 0.819\n   - `extend_best` \u2192 added feature 39 (long), Sharpe 1.055\n   - `extend_best` \u2192 added feature 37 (short), Sharpe 1.151\n\n**Final specification:** Equal-weight combination of **feature 7 (long) + feature 39 (long) + feature 37 (short)**, in-sample annualized Sharpe (net of costs) = **1.1511**.\n\n**Out-of-sample prediction:** mean Sharpe **0.65**, sd **0.40**\n\n**Rationale for the discount:** Although the stated Sharpe is already net of transaction costs, borrow costs, and implementation lag, it was obtained via a greedy forward search that evaluated ~80 candidate features at each step. This kind of selection naturally inflates in-sample performance relative to what a truly new, unseen sample will deliver. I shrunk the point estimate to roughly 55-60% of in-sample and used a wide standard deviation (0.40) to reflect genuine uncertainty about how much of this edge is real signal versus search-induced overfitting, especially since I never got to run `refine` or `pick` to stress-test the sign/feature choices further given the 3-move budget."
